tellraw @a {"text": "Technology: The arch, the vault and the dome", "color": "gold", "bold": true}
tellraw @a {"text": "Arch on centering, finished arch, barrel vault and groin vault.", "color": "white"}
tellraw @a {"text": "Etruscan and Roman, true arch in Etruria 4th century BCE; Roman vaults from 2nd century BCE", "color": "yellow"}
tellraw @a {"text": "Wedge-shaped voussoirs locked by a keystone turn a span into pure compression, which stone", "color": "gray"}
tellraw @a {"text": "and brick carry well. Extruded, the arch becomes a barrel vault; rotated, a dome; two", "color": "gray"}
tellraw @a {"text": "vaults crossing make a groin vault that needs support only at the corners.", "color": "gray"}
tellraw @a {"text": "The arch is built on a timber centering, voussoirs added from both springers until the", "color": "gray"}
tellraw @a {"text": "keystone closes it; only then does it carry load. Its thrust pushes outward, so abutments", "color": "gray"}
tellraw @a {"text": "or buttresses are needed. Roman engineers stacked arcades (Colosseum, Pont du Gard) and", "color": "gray"}
tellraw @a {"text": "used brick ribs in concrete vaults.", "color": "gray"}
tellraw @a {"text": "See it in context: /function ac:italy/rome", "color": "aqua"}
